docs/tables-policy.md:5:The solo UI has no result table. `GameStatus.tsx` displays four properties of a single game in a definition list. Tournament mode adds bracket and past-event result tables; both use captions and column headers under the policy below. Reproduction controls use forms, and the board is a CSS grid of buttons.
docs/competition-research.md:9:The working setup was deliberately modest: one browser game, people seated close enough to see the screen and paper notes beside the device. At the time of this playtest, event sketches were discussed on paper because the game had no participant entry or bracket controller. Tournament mode now supplies those features alongside the ordinary card play and reproduction tools.
docs/dialog-policy.md:5:Solo New Game, Restart, board-code loading and snapshot loading call the controller directly. Tournament's Leave tournament action uses a native dialog before discarding an unfinished event.
docs/dialog-policy.md:9:A confirmation is useful only when it corresponds to a real decision about abandoning work. Its content and focus need to make the conservative path understandable before the player commits to leaving. Leaving unfinished work asks first in a native dialog element with two buttons, Keep playing and Leave tournament. Keep playing takes focus when it opens, and Escape keeps playing. The decision is about the unfinished work being left; opening a disclosure to inspect a code is not itself that decision.
docs/dialog-policy.md:11:The confirmation has two actions: Keep playing and Leave tournament. In particular, the snapshot and Tournament exporters do not turn this confirmation into an automatic save workflow. Export is a separate user action with a text result. The confirmation does not imply that an unsaved draft has been preserved.
docs/dialog-policy.md:13:Tournament owns the current leave route. Its confirmation explains that unfinished progress will be discarded, focuses Keep playing when opened, and treats Escape as Keep playing. Focus policy covers orientation when the Tournament screen replaces the solo controls.
docs/dialog-policy.md:37:A mismatch stores its remaining active milliseconds in game selection. The solo session clock advances it when the page is visible and the imported-pause flag is clear. Tournament uses its separate clock adapter and pauses it while the leave dialog is open.
docs/dialog-policy.md:39:The solo session has no confirmation surface and keeps its existing timing behavior. Tournament pauses its active turn clock while the Leave tournament confirmation is open, including a pending mismatch interval, and resumes with a fresh clock observation when the person keeps playing. Clock design and Mismatch interval explain the established clock model.
docs/dialog-policy.md:49:After a decision, the durability of the source control matters. Existing action buttons generally remain mounted after a deal replacement, while Resume removes itself. Game handles Resume by moving focus before the button is removed. Tournament returns focus to its Leave tournament button when the dialog closes, unless confirming departure replaces the whole screen.
docs/dialog-policy.md:61:Current browser tests establish focus retention on ordinary buttons, invalid draft preservation, Resume's transfer order and stale-timer protection. They do not establish this Tournament dialog journey. Any report of dialog coverage has to point at the corresponding implementation and evidence, rather than citing those tests because they also involve focus.
